Summary: SPMS Client EHR Access Strategy
Purpose
The SPMS workshop focused on defining a scalable, secure access model that allows pharmacists to connect to multiple client (hospital) EHR systems while supporting the upcoming Pittsburgh migration (near-term timeline) without disrupting operations.
________________________________________
Current Challenge
The SPMS team currently relies on a highly fragmented access model, including:
•	Multiple client-to-site VPNs (Cisco, Omnissa, etc.)
•	Client-hosted Citrix environments
•	Direct web-based access
This approach creates:
•	Poor scalability (multiple VPNs per user/device)
•	Operational complexity (manual setup and switching between clients)
•	Inability to access multiple systems concurrently
•	Security risks (uncontrolled external connectivity and identity lifecycle gaps)
________________________________________
Recommended Strategy
1. Standard Target Model (Primary Direction)
Site-to-site VPN or IP Allow-List Access
•	Establish centralized connectivity from corporate/AWS infrastructure to hospital systems
•	Route all access through controlled enterprise channels (no endpoint VPNs)
Benefits:
•	Scalable and repeatable architecture
•	Enables simultaneous multi-client access
•	Aligns with proven enterprise model
________________________________________
2. Preferred Alternative (Where Supported)
IP Allow Listing (Source IP Anchoring)
•	Hospitals restrict access to approved corporate/Zscaler egress IPs
•	Eliminates VPN dependency
Benefits:
•	Fastest to implement
•	Simplest operational model
________________________________________
3. Fallback Solution (For Non-Compliant Clients)
Isolated Access Environments
•	Cloud PC / jump box / lightly managed device
•	Segmented from corporate network
•	Used exclusively for client VPN access
Benefits:
•	Maintains security boundaries
•	Avoids introducing VPN clients onto corporate devices
•	Provides controlled fallback where required
________________________________________
Key Design Principles
•	No client-to-site VPNs on corporate-managed endpoints
•	Maintain Zero Trust posture and network segmentation
•	Avoid sensitive data inspection (EHR traffic)
•	Standardize onboarding toward site-to-site or allow-list models
•	Enable concurrent multi-client access
________________________________________
Risks & Constraints
•	Many hospital partners may lack capabilities for:
o	Site-to-site VPN
o	Federation / SSO
•	Current reliance on local hospital user accounts introduces:
o	Identity lifecycle and offboarding risks
•	Network constraints:
o	IP conflicts (requires NAT strategy)
o	Varying client security requirements
________________________________________
Immediate Priorities (Next 2–4 Weeks)
1. Partner Engagement
•	Contact each hospital IT team to assess:
o	Site-to-site VPN capability
o	IP allow-list support
o	Willingness to adopt preferred model
________________________________________
2. Architecture Definition
•	Finalize:
o	Network design (site-to-site, IP anchoring, fallback paths)
o	NAT and routing strategies
•	Validate compatibility with Zscaler and backend infrastructure
________________________________________
3. Fallback Environment Pilot
•	Stand up and test:
o	Cloud PC / jump box solution
•	Confirm:
o	Security controls (DLP, isolation, monitoring)
o	User experience and performance
________________________________________
4. Migration Readiness
•	Build transition plan:
o	Support parallel access methods temporarily
o	Prioritize critical hospital connections
•	Align timeline to Pittsburgh migration wave
________________________________________
Bottom Line
•	Strategic Direction: Standardize on site-to-site VPN or IP allow-list access
•	Operational Reality: Support a hybrid model short-term
•	Critical Success Factor: Rapid engagement with hospital IT partners + deployment of a secure fallback environment
